[W-NO-VALUES] Warning: `Loop` has no values: every constructor needs a value of `Loop` first
   ╭─[ reject/termination-wrapper-proof-descent-mutual.sw:8:6 ]
   │
 8 │ type Loop: Prop = | MkLoop (next: Loop)
   │      ──┬─                         ──┬─  
   │        ╰──────────────────────────────── this type can never be built
   │                                     │   
   │                                     ╰─── a `Loop` is needed here
   │ 
   │ Note: a value is built from finitely many constructors, so a type whose every constructor holds one of itself is empty. Give a constructor without that field (an end case, as `Nil` is for a list), or, for an endless stream, a function from the position: `Nat -> a`
───╯
[E-TERMINATION] Error: can't show that `f` and `g` terminate
    ╭─[ reject/termination-wrapper-proof-descent-mutual.sw:11:16 ]
    │
 11 │   | MkBox l => g l
    │                ─┬─  
    │                 ╰─── this call to `g` might not terminate: `g`'s 1st argument is smaller than `f`'s 1st argument
    │ 
 13 │   | MkLoop n => f (MkBox n)
    │                 ─────┬────  
    │                      ╰────── then this call to `f`: `f`'s 1st argument, `MkBox n`, isn't seen as smaller than any of `g`'s arguments
    │ 
    │ Note 1: going round `f` → `g` → `f`, over and over, no argument is known to get smaller
    │ 
    │ Note 2: however the recursive calls are repeated, some argument must get smaller (a part of a pattern-matched constructor); an argument passed on unchanged, or rebuilt from its own pattern, is no smaller
    │ 
    │ Note 3: non-terminating definitions are rejected unless they're partial: `{Partial}` in the result type. A depth limit keeps it total and provable: an extra `fuel: Nat` argument that decreases on each call, failing (or returning a default) when it runs out
